{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // Viewer as a Cloudflare Worker with Static Assets — Cloudflare's 2026
  // recommended static-hosting platform. Fully separate from the CMS worker, and
  // NEVER named "run-apparel" (the separate live commercial site).
  //
  // ✅ CUTOVER DONE (2026-07-22): this worker serves viewer.wear-run.help (custom
  // domain attached in the dashboard). The old "run-apparel-viewer" Pages
  // project has been DELETED. CI deploys here because the VIEWER_DEPLOY_TARGET
  // repo variable is "worker". Rollback to Pages: set VIEWER_DEPLOY_TARGET=pages
  // (CI recreates the Pages project automatically), then attach
  // viewer.wear-run.help to it in the dashboard — see docs/RUNBOOK.md →
  // "Viewer: Pages → Worker cutover".
  "name": "run-apparel-viewer-site",

  /**
   * ⚠️ THIS WORKER SERVES viewer.wear-run.help, AND THAT BINDING IS NOT DECLARED HERE.
   *
   * `apps/cms` and `infra/apex-404` both carry a `routes` block naming their hostname;
   * this one — the address printed on physical QR tags — is attached out-of-band, so a
   * rebuild from a blank account would deploy a Worker that answers on nothing.
   * Audit 2026-08-30 PM, finding L5-09.
   *
   * ⚠️ DO NOT ADD A `routes` BLOCK WITHOUT READING THE LIVE ONE FIRST. wrangler
   * RECONCILES routes to this file — the warning `infra/apex-404/wrangler.jsonc:9`
   * already carries — so a block that does not match what is live DETACHES the Worker
   * from the hostname, and every QR tag already sewn into a garment stops resolving.
   *
   * ⚠️ READ 2026-08-31, AND THE ANSWER CHANGES THE ADVICE. This block used to say the
   * live configuration "could not be READ" because the zone token answered
   * `Authentication error`. That was the TOKEN, not the account. With a key that can
   * read routes:
   *
   *   GET /zones/<id>/workers/routes        -> www.wear-run.help/*  and  wear-run.help/*
   *                                            BOTH -> run-apparel-apex-404
   *   GET /accounts/<id>/workers/domains?service=run-apparel-viewer-site
   *                                         -> viewer.wear-run.help
   *
   * So this Worker has NO ROUTE AT ALL. It is bound by a **Custom Domain**, which is
   * an account-scoped resource and a different mechanism — the only routes on the zone
   * belong to the apex Worker. That makes "add a matching `routes` block" the WRONG
   * fix, not merely a risky one: a `routes` entry here would add a binding beside the
   * custom domain rather than describe it, and wrangler's reconciliation is what then
   * makes the failure mode "every printed tag goes dark".
   *
   * Leave it out. The binding is recorded here in prose, which is what L5-09 actually
   * asked for — the address written down where the next person looks. If it is ever
   * declared in config, the field is `routes: [{ pattern, custom_domain: true }]`, and
   * it must be deployed against a confirmed `https://viewer.wear-run.help/rxps/wine`
   * 200 with the garment payload — a plain GET, never a HEAD.
   */
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  // ⚠️ MISSING UNTIL 2026-08-30, ON THE ONLY PUBLIC-FACING WORKER. `apps/cms`,
  // `apps/shrink` and `infra/apex-404` all declared this; the viewer — the one a
  // customer's QR scan actually lands on — did not, so its `console.warn`s and any
  // uncaught exception went nowhere queryable. Free tier, and the sampling defaults
  // are right for this volume; do not add head_sampling_rate without a measurement.
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  // The internal workers.dev URL and per-version preview URLs are disabled:
  // visitors only ever use viewer.wear-run.help. (Re-enable workers_dev
  // temporarily if you need a pre-cutover smoke-test URL.)
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,
  // Worker + static assets. It was assets-ONLY until 2026-08-08; the script does
  // exactly one thing — rewrite the Open Graph tags per garment so a link shared
  // with a lead unfurls as that garment in that colourway. See worker/index.ts.
  //
  // ⚠️ `run_worker_first` IS AN ARRAY, AND THE ARRAY FORM IS THE WHOLE POINT.
  //
  // It was ABSENT until 2026-09-07, on the reasoning that adding it puts the Worker in
  // front of every asset request and `/assets/*.js` would lose the zero-overhead path it
  // had before the script existed. That reasoning was sound and the conclusion was wrong,
  // because it was reasoning about `run_worker_first: true`. Cloudflare's docs for
  // Advanced routing control (wrangler >= 4.20.0; this repo pins 4.140.0):
  //
  //   "you can use run_worker_first with an array of route patterns. This approach
  //    DISABLES the automatic Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate detection and gives you explicit
  //    control over which requests should be handled by your Worker script vs served as
  //    static assets."
  //
  // Two facts in one sentence. The automatic navigate detection is what was skipping the
  // Worker; an array both turns that off and lets the asset paths keep bypassing it. So
  // the cost that argued against `true` does not apply to this shape.
  //
  // ⚠️ WHAT IT FIXES — MEASURED AGAINST THE LIVE VIEWER 2026-09-07, one header apart
  // (audit FA-N-14, FA-N-15):
  //
  //   /rxps/wine   Twitterbot, plain              200, og:title "R-XPS X-MILO PRO SKIN-SUIT"
  //   /rxps/wine   Twitterbot + navigate          200, og:title "RUN APPAREL — 3D Product…"
  //   /manifest.webmanifest  browser, plain       404
  //   /manifest.webmanifest  browser + navigate   200
  //
  // A NAVIGATION IS THE ONLY WAY A HUMAN OR A LINK CRAWLER EVER REACHES A PAGE, so both
  // features the Worker exists for — the per-garment card and the soft-404 fix shipped on
  // 2026-09-04 — were inert for their entire audience. Neither was broken code; the
  // script was never invoked.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT OF THIS SAID THE OPPOSITE, because node's `fetch`
  // REWRITES `sec-fetch-mode` to `cors` (it is a forbidden header name). Every "navigate"
  // column read identical to plain, which reads as "already fixed". Verified against a
  // local echo server before any of the numbers above were believed, and taken with a raw
  // `node:https` client. Do not measure this with `fetch`.
  //
  // ⚠️ NEGATIVE PATTERNS TAKE PRECEDENCE AND ORDER IS INSIGNIFICANT (max 100 entries).
  // The exclusions below are the paths that are always real files, so they keep the
  // asset-router path exactly as before. `/og/*` is deliberately NOT excluded — the
  // Worker turns a missing preview image from a 200 HTML page into a 404, and a social
  // platform asking for that image is precisely who needs it.
  //
  // ⚠️ A PATH NOT LISTED HERE IS NOT BROKEN, ONLY SLOWER: the default branch returns
  // `env.ASSETS.fetch(request)`, which is also what keeps `dist/_headers` on the
  // response — it is applied by the static asset handler, so a response pulled through
  // the binding carries the CSP and HSTS and a `new Response(...)` carries neither
  // (measured on the live edge 2026-08-12).
  //
  // ⚠️ VERIFY AFTER DEPLOY, NOT BEFORE. This is a routing change on the one Worker a
  // printed QR tag lands on, and no local runtime proves the edge. Re-run the two rows
  // above against viewer.wear-run.help and expect the navigate rows to match the plain
  // ones. `scripts/smoke-viewer-preview.mjs` carries the crawler assertion.
  "main": "./worker/index.ts",
  // `single-page-application` replaces the Pages `_redirects` SPA fallback (CI
  // deletes dist/_redirects for this target — Workers Static Assets rejects the
  // "/* /index.html 200" rule); dist/_headers (immutable asset caching + the CSP)
  // is honoured natively, and was measured on 2026-08-08 to survive a response
  // pulled through the ASSETS binding below — the open question that had blocked
  // adding a Worker here at all.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "run_worker_first": [
      "/*",
      "!/assets/*",
      "!/basis/*",
      "!/draco/*",
      "!/env/*",
      "!/apple-touch-icon.png",
      "!/favicon.ico",
      "!/favicon.svg",
      "!/llms.txt",
      "!/meshopt_decoder.js",
      "!/og-default.jpg",
      "!/robots.txt",
      "!/sitemap.xml",
      "!/sw.js"
    ]
  },
  // Internal, same-account call to the CMS worker for the published payload. A
  // service binding rather than a public fetch: no DNS, no TLS, no zone in the
  // path — so this cannot be challenged by a bot rule the way cms.wear-run.help
  // has been, and it costs nothing at the edge.
  "services": [
    {
      "binding": "CMS",
      "service": "run-apparel-viewer-cms"
    }
  ]
}
